---
// Section root for Palm Central.
//
// Deliberately not the page palmcentral.dst.llc opens with. That one is a
// buyer's guide: it asks which phase is on sale and what to put to a sales
// contact. This one answers the question a reader of a property hub asks
// first — how far along is the island this flat is being sold on — and it
// can answer it because Nakheel publishes the percentages itself. The two
// pages share a subject and no sentences.
import VerticalLayout from "../../layouts/VerticalLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "@dst/ui/PageHero.astro";
import Callout from "@dst/ui/Callout.astro";
import Faq from "@dst/ui/Faq.astro";

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: "How far along is Palm Jebel Ali?",
    a: "Nakheel's own construction-progress page puts the island at <strong>26.75%</strong> overall. The number is not one thing: substructure is at 93.27% and building work at 21.64%, which is the difference between an island that exists and homes that can be lived in.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is Palm Central?",
    a: "The first apartment product on Palm Jebel Ali, on the Spine rather than a frond. The second phase, reported by The National on 24 June 2026, put <strong>222 homes</strong> on sale from <strong>AED 2.7 million</strong>, in three low-rise buildings, for handover in <strong>September 2030</strong>.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is the first phase still available?",
    a: "No. Phase 1 released in October 2025 and sold out; what trades now is resale. Most third-party listings still quote its AED 2.5 million entry price, which is why a headline figure and a purchase price can differ by AED 200,000 for the same size of flat.",
  },
  {
    q: "How big is Palm Jebel Ali compared with Palm Jumeirah?",
    a: "About twice the size — <strong>13.4 square kilometres</strong>, seven islands and sixteen fronds, adding more than 90km of beachfront. Sheikh Mohammed bin Rashid Al Maktoum announced the relaunch on 31 May 2023.",
  },
];
---
<VerticalLayout
  vertical="palm-central"
  prose
  title="Palm Central, Dubai: what the island's own numbers say"
  description="Nakheel puts Palm Jebel Ali at 26.75% built, and publishes the split. What that means for Palm Central — the Dubai island's first apartments, from AED 2.7 million, handover September 2030."
  image="/covers/pc-section.jpg"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Palm Central"
    title="Palm Central, Dubai: what the island's own numbers say"
    image="/covers/pc-section.jpg"
    imageAlt="Cut-paper illustration: a broad flat causeway running out from the edge of the frame into open water, a low ridge and a pale sun behind it."
    imageKind="generated"
  >
    <p class="lede">
      Most off-plan property in Dubai asks a buyer to take a completion date
      on trust. Palm Jebel Ali is unusual: Nakheel publishes the progress
      percentages itself, and they currently read <strong>26.75%</strong>
      overall — <strong>93.27%</strong> of substructure against
      <strong>21.64%</strong> of building work. Palm Central, the island's
      first apartments, is being sold into that gap, for handover in
      <strong>September 2030</strong>.
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>What the single percentage hides</h2>
    <p>
      An island is built in an order, and a blended figure averages across
      stages that are nowhere near each other. Nakheel's breakdown:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Substructure 93.27%</strong> — the land itself, effectively done.</li>
      <li><strong>Superstructure 68.40%</strong> — frames going up.</li>
      <li><strong>Infrastructure 65.05%</strong> — roads, services, the things that make an address reachable.</li>
      <li><strong>Building work 21.64%</strong> — the part a resident would recognise.</li>
      <li><strong>MEP 18.76%</strong> — mechanical, electrical and plumbing, the last long job before a handover date means anything.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Read in that order the figure stops being a progress bar and becomes a
      sequence: the hard civil engineering is behind, and four years of
      finishing is what the September 2030 date is actually made of.
    </p>

    <h2>Palm Central is on the Spine, not a frond</h2>
    <p>
      The fronds of Palm Jebel Ali are villa land. The Spine is the
      through-road, and it is where Nakheel has put the community-scale
      buildings — which is why the island's only apartments are there. By
      frond, Nakheel's inspection of <strong>10 March 2026</strong> recorded
      Frond M at <strong>22.10%</strong> and Frond N at
      <strong>29.20%</strong>; those are Palm Central's neighbours, and their
      numbers are the best available answer to what the view will be doing
      while you wait.
    </p>

    <h2>The scale it sits inside</h2>
    <p>
      Palm Jebel Ali covers <strong>13.4 square kilometres</strong>, roughly
      twice Palm Jumeirah, across <strong>seven islands</strong> and
      <strong>sixteen fronds</strong>, adding more than
      <strong>90km of beachfront</strong> and around 110km of coastline to
      Dubai. In April 2026 The National reported Nakheel awarding contracts
      worth <strong>AED 3.5 billion</strong> for <strong>544 villas</strong>,
      split between two contractors across Fronds A to F. Three low-rise
      buildings on the Spine are an early and small piece of that.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Callout kind="warn">
    <p>
      Two entry prices circulate for this project and only one is current.
      <strong>AED 2.5 million</strong> is Phase 1, released October 2025 and
      sold out; <strong>AED 2.7 million</strong> is Phase 2, the inventory
      actually on sale. A listing quoting the lower number without saying
      which release it belongs to is quoting a flat that is no longer for
      sale by the developer.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/palm-central/prices/" title="Palm Central prices in Dubai, by unit type and phase">What the price ladder says about the product</a> — and where AED 2.7 million sits in Dubai's 2026 market.</li>
      <li><a href="/palm-central/payment-plan/" title="The Palm Central payment plan in Dubai">Four years of instalments</a>, and what "construction-linked" means when the developer publishes its own progress.</li>
      <li><a href="/palm-central/location/" title="Where Palm Central sits on Palm Jebel Ali, Dubai">The Spine, the fronds either side, and the airport question</a>.</li>
      <li><a href="/palm-central/golden-visa/" title="Palm Central and the Dubai Golden Visa threshold">Every unit clears the AED 2 million threshold</a> — which is the start of the question, not the end.</li>
      <li><a href="/palm-central/faq/" title="Palm Central questions, answered">The questions buyers actually ask</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Dubai's wider market is on <a href="/news/" title="Dubai property, tech and AI news">the news feed</a>, the residency route is on
      <a href="/visas/golden/" title="Golden Visa in Dubai through property">the Golden Visa page</a>,
      and the project's own enquiry pages sit on
      <a href="https://palmcentral.dst.llc" title="Palm Central buyer's guide">palmcentral.dst.llc</a>.
      If you would rather talk to someone,
      <a href="/contact/" title="Get in touch with DST in Dubai">contact is here</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqItems} />

  <section class="container">
    <p class="source-note">
      Progress percentages, the component split and the per-frond figures of
      10 March 2026: Nakheel, construction progress for Palm Jebel Ali, read
      22 September 2026. Phase 2 unit count, entry price and handover: The
      National, 24 June 2026. Island area, island and frond counts,
      beachfront and the 31 May 2023 relaunch, and the AED 3.5 billion villa
      contracts of April 2026: The National and Nakheel. Nothing here is
      investment advice.
    </p>
  </section>
</VerticalLayout>
